--TEST--
An enum converts to its own backing type and to nothing else, at `as string`, at `echo`, inside an interpolation and at the backing type the other enum was declared with (`rule:enums/no-class-machinery`, `rule:types/conversion`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:enums/a-case-is-its-integer
// `rule:enums/closed-integer-type` makes a case a named integer, so every
// conversion out of one is a question about a *number*. Five spellings ask for
// something else, and each is refused where it is written: `as string` and the
// two implicit string conversions, because a case has no text form at all, and
// the two crossings between `int` and `uint`, because a case converts to the
// backing type its own enum was declared with and to no other. `$case as int`
// is the one spelling that answers, and `($case as int) as string` is the text.
enum Rank {
    Bronze,
    Silver,
}

enum Mask: uint {
    None = 0,
    All = 7,
}

Rank $held = Rank::Silver;
echo Rank::Silver as string, "\n";
echo Rank::Silver, "\n";
echo "rank $held\n";
echo Rank::Silver as uint, "\n";
echo Mask::All as int, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0708]: `Rank` cannot be converted to `string`
%A
error[E0707]: `Rank` has no string form
%A
error[E0707]: `Rank` has no string form
%A
error[E0708]: `Rank` cannot be converted to `uint`
%A
error[E0708]: `Mask` cannot be converted to `int`
%A
error: aborting due to 5 errors
